---
import Gamescale from '../../components/Gamescale.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'gamescale gives each game your screen’s real resolution under GNOME fractional scaling, and puts the scale back when it quits, even if the game dies.';

const FLATPAK_ERR =
  '$ flatpak run --command=sh --filesystem=/usr/bin/gamescale:ro com.valvesoftware.Steam\nF: Not sharing "/usr/bin/gamescale" with sandbox: Path "/usr" is reserved by Flatpak';
const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'gamescale',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/gamescale', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const SETUP = 'pulsar setup gamescale --platform steam';
const TOC = [
  { id: 'how', label: 'How it works', level: 2 },
  { id: 'in-the-image', label: 'What ships in the image', level: 2 },
  { id: 'flatpak', label: 'Flatpak launchers', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="gamescale"
  fullTitle="gamescale — native-resolution games with fractional scaling"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/gamescale"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <p class="above"><img class="gs-icon" src="/assets/gamescale.svg" alt="" width="48" height="48" /></p>
  <h1>gamescale</h1>
  <p class="intro">Your screen’s real resolution, for one game at a time.</p>

  <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
  <Gamescale />

  <h2 id="in-the-image">What ships in the image</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/arclight-digital/gamescale">gamescale</a> ships at a pinned,
    hash-verified tag: the script at <code>/usr/bin/gamescale</code>, its top-bar indicator enabled
    system-wide, and a reconcile unit that restores your scale if a game dies without cleaning up.
  </p>
  <p>
    For <strong>native</strong> launchers and terminal use that is everything, and a machine that
    never installs anything still recovers its scale after a crash.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flatpak">Flatpak launchers need one more step</h2>
  <p>
    For <strong>Flatpak</strong> launchers, Steam included, it is not enough, and no setting can
    make it enough. Flatpak reserves <code>/usr</code> for its own runtime and refuses to share the
    host’s:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="flatpak" code={FLATPAK_ERR}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    The <code>/usr</code> a Flatpak sees is the runtime’s, so no grant can ever expose{' '}
    <code>/usr/bin/gamescale</code> to Steam. A copy under <code>$HOME</code> is the only kind that
    maps in, which means granting a launcher always means running the installer:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="setup" code={SETUP}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    That runs the installer copy staged in the image: offline, pinned, matching the image exactly.
    Upstream’s <code>curl | sh</code> works too; it just fetches whatever is current rather than what
    the image pinned.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The install shadows the image copy, and that is the intended outcome.</strong> User
    paths win every collision: your shell finds <code>~/.local/bin</code> before{' '}
    <code>/usr/bin</code>, GNOME prefers a user extension over a system one with the same uuid, and
    systemd prefers a user unit over <code>/usr/lib/systemd/user</code>. One of each runs, never
    both.
  </p>
  <p class="hint">
    Chasing odd behavior? <code>gamescale --version</code> tells you which copy you are actually
    running.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .above {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .gs-icon {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
